// Flattening: every contour of the outline `tt_outline` just produced
// becomes a run of straight edges.
//
// The four lists this reads -- ttends, ttpx, ttpy, ttpon -- are re-declared
// by every tt_outline (docs/FRICTION.md 13), so they are imported at the
// point of use and never held. That is why no function here takes them as a
// parameter.
ras_flat() {
  int k = 0;
  while(k < len((import ttends))) {
    k = ras_ctr(k);
  }
} return void;

// Contour k runs from ras_lo(k) through ttends[k] inclusive: the file stores
// only the last index of each contour, so the first is one past the previous
// contour's last, and 0 for the first contour.
ras_lo(int k) {
  int lo = 0;
  if(k > 0) {
    lo = (import ttends)[k - 1] + 1;
  }
} return int lo;

// One contour. buf is the whole of the pen's state, because `id` has no
// record and a quadratic pen carries seven numbers at once -- see
// ctr/open.id for what each cell means.
ras_ctr(int k) {
  int[] buf = [];
  ras_open(buf, ras_lo(k), (import ttends)[k]);
  ras_run(buf);
} return int k + 1;
